# Math
@summary Stdlib Math — trig, exp/log, rounding, conversions, gcd, clamp
@category stdlib

The Math module provides scalar numeric primitives. Trigonometry takes radians (use radians(deg) to convert). Floating-point functions return Double; integer-flavored functions like floor/ceil/round return Long.

There is no built-in pi constant — define it yourself if needed (the Kuramoto demo uses pure twoPi() -> float = () { 6.283185307179586 }).

## Trigonometry

sin(x), cos(x), tan(x) — radians in, double out.

asin(x), acos(x), atan(x) — inverses; results in radians.

atan2(y, x) — full-range arctangent. Note the y-first argument order (matches C/Java).

radians(deg), degrees(rad) — angle conversions.

## Exponentials and powers

exp(x), log(x), log10(x) — natural and base-10 exponentials.

pow(base, exponent) — general power. sqrt(x), cbrt(x) — square and cube roots.

hypot(x, y) — the Euclidean length without intermediate overflow: sqrt(x*x + y*y) done safely.

heron(a, b, c) — triangle area from side lengths via Heron's formula.

## Rounding and conversion

floor(x), ceil(x), round(x) — Long results.

toInt(x), toFloat(x) — explicit conversions between Long and Double. Use toFloat in arithmetic that mixes ints and floats.

abs(x), sign(x) — magnitude and -1/0/1 sign.

## Bit operations

shl(value, count), shr(value, count) — logical shift left/right on Long.

bitWidth(value) — minimum number of bits needed to represent the value (i.e., position of highest set bit).

## Numeric helpers

min(a, b), max(a, b) — pairwise min and max. (For min/max of an array, use arrayMin / arrayMax from Array.)

clamp(value, lo, hi) — pin value into [lo, hi].

gcd(a, b) — greatest common divisor.

random() — uniform Double in [0.0, 1.0).

--- example: trig.spn
-- Compute coordinates around a unit circle at 30-degree increments.
import Math (cos, sin, radians)
import Array (range, map)

let angles = map(range(0, 13), do(i) { radians(toFloat(i) * 30.0) })
map(angles, do(t) { (cos(t), sin(t)) })
-- 13 (cos, sin) pairs from 0° to 360°
---

--- example: random-stats.spn
-- Generate a sample, then compute mean and standard deviation.
import Math (random, sqrt, toFloat)
import Array (range, map, sum, length)

pure squared(float) -> float = (x) { x * x }

let n = 100
let xs = map(range(0, n), do(i) { random() })

let mean = sum(xs) / toFloat(length(xs))
let centered = map(xs, do(x) { x - mean })
let variance = sum(map(centered, squared)) / toFloat(length(xs))
let stddev = sqrt(variance)

(mean, stddev)
---

--- example: clamp-and-lerp.spn
-- Build a few helpers on top of Math primitives.
import Math (clamp, toFloat)

pure lerp(float, float, float) -> float = (a, b, t) {
    a + (b - a) * t
}

pure remap(float, float, float, float, float) -> float = (x, fromLo, fromHi, toLo, toHi) {
    let t = clamp((x - fromLo) / (fromHi - fromLo), 0.0, 1.0)
    lerp(toLo, toHi, t)
}

-- Map a slider value (0..100) to a hue (0..360) clipped on input.
remap(75.0, 0.0, 100.0, 0.0, 360.0)
---

--- example: integer-math.spn
-- Bit ops and gcd on integers.
import Math (gcd, shl, shr, bitWidth)

let a = gcd(48, 36)
let b = shl(1, 10)
let c = shr(1024, 3)
let d = bitWidth(255)

(a, b, c, d)
-- (12, 1024, 128, 8)
